# @@service_name@@

Kafka consumer for `@@topic@@`, owned by `@@team@@`. Generated by `paved new` from the
`kafka-consumer` template, so it ships with the platform defaults already wired in.

## What you get without writing any code

| Concern | Default |
| --- | --- |
| Delivery | At least once. Offsets commit after a message is handled or dead lettered. |
| Failures | Bounded retries with backoff, then `@@dlq_topic@@` with reason headers. |
| Logs | JSON on stdout with `trace_id` and `span_id` of the active span. |
| Metrics | `/metrics`: `messages_processed_total`, `message_processing_seconds`, `kafka_consumer_lag`, `dead_letter_messages_total`. |
| Traces | One consumer span per message, continuing the producer's W3C trace context. |
| Health | `/healthz` fails if the poll loop stalls, `/readyz` passes once the pod joins the group. |
| Security | Non root, read only root filesystem, no capabilities, NetworkPolicy, own ServiceAccount. |
| Scaling | KEDA scales on consumer lag (2 to @@max_replicas@@ replicas). |

## Develop

```bash
pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
pytest
paved check deploy/k8s        # same policy gate CI runs
```

Put your logic in `app/handler.py`. Raise `PermanentError` for messages that can never
succeed. Handlers must be idempotent, because a crash replays uncommitted messages.

## Operate

Alerts for this service link to the platform runbooks: consumer lag, error budget burn,
and crash loops. `paved runbook triage KafkaConsumerLagHigh --label namespace=@@namespace@@`
prints the playbook with this service's names filled in.
